commit ef81c1cac0992799f906c514b1700f5fff5be672
Author:     Brendan Abolivier <brendan@thunderbird.net>
AuthorDate: Wed Jul 29 17:34:52 2026 +0000
Commit:     toby@thunderbird.net <toby@thunderbird.net>
CommitDate: Wed Jul 29 17:37:01 2026 +0000

    Bug 2050347 - Defer to Necko's implementation of HTTP auth for Exchange requests. r=edicharry

    NTLM authentication seems to stick to TCP connections rather than IP addresses.
    We don't have access to the connection pool directly, so we cannot ensure, from
    the Rust/Thunderbird side of things, that connections are properly authenticated
    before we use them - or, more importantly, that a message 3 in the
    authentication process is sent down the same connection we received the message
    2 through, potentially causing authentication issues.

    Necko does have working implementations of NTLM and Basic auth, though, as long
    as we insert the right credentials in its auth cache. The authentication process
    for a request runs on a single connection, so we don't end up clashing while
    trying to authenticate across multiple connections, meaning the parallelism
    provided by the operation queue stays unaffected.

    This patch gets rid of the NTLM and Basic authentication implementations, and
    instead sets the authentication cache using the server's URL and credentials. It
    comes with a couple of caveats that are documented in TODO comments which we'll
    hopefully be able to address shortly.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D314686

